# Optimization Under Uncertainty

In real world autonomy, we never have perfect information. Sensors have noise, and other agents (pedestrians, cars) have intent that is fundamentally unpredictable. Thus, we change our goals from finding a single "optimal" path to finding paths that are robust and safe across a distribution of possible futures.


## Types of uncertainty

- Additive Noise i.e gaussian noise or sensor drift
- Model Mismatch i.e. an incorrect physics model
- Parametric Uncertainty
- Structural uncertainty (will an agent in the environment respond with x or y)

## Stochastic Programming & Chance Constraints

Instead of hard constraints ($Gx \leq h$), we use **Chance** contraints, where we accept a small risk that a constraint is violated.

$$
\min f_0(x) \\
\\\text {}
\\
\text{subject to: } P(f_i(x, \omega) > 0 ) \leq \epsilon
$$

where $\omega$ is a random variable. This effectively means the probability of hitting an obstacle must be less than 1%. 

## Deterministic Conversion

To solve this, we convert the probabilistic contraint into a deterministic equivalent. If the uncertainty is Gaussian ($\mathcal{N}(\mu, \sigma)$), we can "tighten" the constraints by some factor of the variance. This is basically creating a safety buffer around obstacles. 

## Robust Optimization (Minimax)

This optimizes for the worst case scenario within an uncertainty set

$$
\min{x} \max{w \in U} f(x, \omega)
$$

## Tube MPC

In Tube MPC, we create an envelope around a central ideal trajectory, and ensure that the whole tube is collision free

## Solving Techniques

If we can't solve analytically, we can sample N possible realizations and solve for an optimal path in all scenarios

